And so in this case, we have an 11 labs music skill that helps explain the API.

493
00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:26.400
And so when we run, when we use that we indeed

494
00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:28.360
see that for our dynamic soundtrack generator.

495
00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:30.520
And I'm using Tessl here to do the evaluations.

496
00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:32.960
You see that without context it didn't do a bunch of things.

497
00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:33.600
It did.

498
00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:37.240
It used the deprecated package, it didn't import it correctly and all that.

499
00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:39.760
And with the skill with the context, it got it done.

500
00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:44.680
So it got a 50% average without the skill, versus 98% with it.

501
00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:46.480
So it's good we managed to correct it.

502
00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:51.880
And if we run this across the line, we see in some cases we just solved it.

503
00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:56.480
It just basically now is sufficiently good to fairly consistently do the task.

504
00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:59.880
And in other cases it's still slightly varied, but it's further up,

505
00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:02.880
so it is more capable of doing that task.

506
00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:04.960
So this is the type of way in which you evolve

507
00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:07.640
and you improve your agents ability to do something.

508
00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:08.560
This is a security event.

509
00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:10.400
So how do we talk about this for security?

510
00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:11.840
Well, let's take another example.

511
00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:14.360
Code guard is something that Cisco created and donated.

512
00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:17.640
And it's basically a bunch of sort of OWASP security

513
00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.560
kind of rules packaged up into a skill.

514
00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:24.560
And and it helps kind of these

515
00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:28.040
AI developers, these agents develop more secure code.

516
00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:30.200
So let's put that to the test.

517
00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:33.760
I created six different evaluation scenarios.

518
00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:35.800
And I specifically focused on authorization.

519
00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:38.520
How well did you handle the security of authorization in this code.

520
00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:41.640
So for instance hey agent, create an access control

521
00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:46.440
test suite for a project management API and then created a bunch of scores for it.

522
00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:48.760
And I did it with and without code Guard.

523
00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:50.960
And as you would expect, it got better.

524
00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:55.520
So without Code Guard, just as is, the agent scored 48%

525
00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:59.720
on my kind of authorization criteria, sort of a scorecard.

526
00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:00.760
So not awesome.

527
00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:05.680
And if it had these instructions nearly 1.6 x the improvement on.

528
00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:08.240
So it got a fair bit better because there was a bunch of guides

529
00:27:08.240 --> 00:27:11.360
about how to sort of code securely, including authorization.

530
00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:14.000
So that's that's good. That's already useful.

531
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.960
However, if I took all the information in Code Guard

532
00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:17.320
that has a

533
00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:21.160
lot of security practices in it, and I shrunk it down to just the

534
00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:25.200
authentication and authorisation related bits, which are, I think about sort of 5%

535
00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:26.040
of the total content.

536
00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:28.440
If I remember correctly, it did a lot better.

537
00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:29.760
It went to 98%.

538
00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:32.240
And this is this is sort of a core thing to remember,

539
00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:34.160
which is more context is not necessarily better. Right.

540
00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:37.960
If I sat here and I told you 100 things, no matter how sort of brilliant or dumb

541
00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:38.680
they were,

542
00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:42.440
you will notice you'll give less attention to each one of them than if I told you.

543
00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:46.440
Three and attention is a scarce resource with humans and with models.

544
00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:47.920
And so choosing and managing.

545
00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:51.640
What is it that you say that actually matters that the model doesn't

546
00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:52.360
already know,

547
00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:55.360
like not wasteful information that there's no point in saying

548
00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:57.760
is important and is a part of that competency.

549
00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:00.640
Taking that a little bit further,

550
00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:00.960
you know,

551
00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:02.920
if this is the graph that shows the same numbers

552
00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:06.000
from before of code guard, not guard, you also might be surprised

553
00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:09.440
to hear the different agents respond to the same information differently.

554
00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:11.200
So this is an example of the same test.

555
00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:14.400
In fact a whole it's literally the same execution.

556
00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.720
And what you can see is different models respond differently.

557
00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:20.120
Opus and sonnet at the top they get about the same results.

558
00:28:20.120 --> 00:28:23.040
Even though opus is more intelligent, it is more expensive.

559
00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.840
So if you use the opus for this specific task, you kind of wasted money

560
00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:28.560
and probably time because you could do the same with sonnet.

561
00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:30.920
And what you can see is codex and cursor.

562
00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:34.520
They respond even differently and it doesn't matter which one is better.

563
00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:35.640
Like it does matter eventually.

564
00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:38.720
But that's not my point, but rather

565
00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:42.640
the fact that different agents, again, almost like humans, listen differently.

566
00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:44.520
And so you want to know that your instructions

567
00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:48.280
are effective, are not wasteful for the agents that you are using.

568
00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:52.640
So that's kind of core point here is if we want to secure

569
00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:56.080
agents, we want to use skills to help them secure code or write secure code.

570
00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:58.840
We have to learn how to measure that.

571
00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:00.800
And how do you how do you build good context.

572
00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:03.160
Like how do you how do you evolve it?

573
00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.960
How do you create kind of quality context, you know, how to build.

574
00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:07.080
So where to talk a little bit

575
00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:10.360
about the fact that you generate you create a skill, you evaluate it,

576
00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:12.640
and then once you've evaluated, you can optimize it

577
00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:15.640
until you kind of get a better and better guidance for that agent.

578
00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:18.040
And then you need to distribute that or communicate it.

579
00:29:18.040 --> 00:29:22.400
If you want to use the human mode to the agents and observe what has happened.

580
00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:23.480
And the observe is important.

581
00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:26.720
If the evals are kind of like your tests, once you've got something working

582
00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:29.240
and you make a modification, how do you know you're not breaking it?

583
00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:31.120
How do you know you're evolving it?

584
00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:33.400
How do you know if you can use a cheaper model or not?

585
00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:35.240
You have to be able to evaluate, but eventually

586
00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:37.040
your test will go out of out of sync.

587
00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:39.560
They would not represent reality if you don't also observe

588
00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:40.120
what is happening.

589
00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:42.560
So we call this the context development lifecycle.

590
00:29:42.560 --> 00:29:43.480
And as you build

591
00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:48.080
good context, you can use that context across the software development lifecycle.

592
00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:50.920
So we think the CDLC is where us humans should live.

593
00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:53.600
We should be building good context that guides the agents.

594
00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:56.800
And then we should apply that to the SDLC where the agents should work.

595
00:29:56.800 --> 00:29:57.840
And the same context,

596
00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:02.560
the same instruction can be applied end to end in the development process.

597
00:30:02.920 --> 00:30:05.680
Same as like a great developer on the team will use the same knowledge

598
00:30:05.680 --> 00:30:08.680
to define a product feature, write the code, troubleshoot something,

599
00:30:08.680 --> 00:30:12.480
ship it to production, troubleshoot an incident, etc. etc.

600
00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:14.240
the same knowledge is useful

601
00:30:14.240 --> 00:30:16.600
across the board, so the skills represent that knowledge.

602
00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:19.080
And of course, from a security lens perspective,

603
00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:22.360
we can now use that to secure different steps.

604
00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:26.560
So this is this is how you should write secure code at the beginning.

605
00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:29.600
This is what I want to audit in the code review to highlight to you.

606
00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:31.400
This is what I want to get on.

607
00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:33.840
This is what I want to inspect when an incident occurred.

608
00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:37.520
So all of these things can be represented in skills that we use across the SDLC.

609
00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:41.600
So this is non-deterministic.

610
00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:43.840
It's kind of my biggest point to make.

611
00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:46.400
And as we as we learn how to evaluate those.

612
00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:47.880
The second point is

613
00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:49.840
we've been talking more and more about these skills

614
00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:52.560
and we're sort of optimizing these skills and we're developing these skills.

615
00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:53.800
And I think it's useful to start

616
00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:58.360
thinking about skills in terms of their own security as a unit of software.

617
00:30:58.400 --> 00:30:59.720
Right. It looked like a markdown file.

618
00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:02.720
They look like a notion document or like a confluence document.

619
00:31:02.720 --> 00:31:06.600
But the way we process them is we execute them by the agent

620
00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:07.840
or the agent execute them.

621
00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:10.840
So really, I think we're well served, especially from a security lens,

622
00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:15.840
to think about them as a unit of software, not just as a piece of text.

623
00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:18.680
And we have like a lot of indications of that today.

624
00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:18.840
Right?

625
00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:21.200
We have the Snyk study, and there were very many others

626
00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:23.160
that showed, especially in the open Claude world,

627
00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:26.880
where a lot of skills were malicious, literally attackers putting in things

628
00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:30.600
that are trying to make the, the, the agent to do something it shouldn't.

629
00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:32.640
Here's an example of a malicious skill.

630
00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:33.840
This is from the Tessl

631
00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:37.440
registry scanned by Snyk, where it had a bunch of that it down.

632
00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:40.640
And while most of your URLs were just standard blockchain

633
00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:43.760
APIs, one of them was suddenly downloading a password protected zip.

634
00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:45.360
Fishy doesn't sound right.

635
00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:47.040
Okay, that's potentially a malicious skill.

636
00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:47.760
There's a bunch of things

637
00:31:47.760 --> 00:31:51.240
we can do, clearly imperfect, but we can try to detect malicious skills.

638
00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:53.560
There are also vulnerable skills.

639
00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:54.680
What's a vulnerable skill?

640
00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:58.240
For instance, a skill that uses insecure credential handling.

641
00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:02.440
It asks the user to put API keys inside, or it makes MCP calls

642
00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:05.760
with sort of plain vanilla tokens for it.

643
00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:08.040
So that's an example of something that is insecure behavior.

644
00:32:08.040 --> 00:32:12.080
It's vulnerable to exfiltrate some information outside.

645
00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:17.000
There's also like new types of flaws that you might have in what I

646
00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:20.840
this is not an industry term, but what I like to think of as negligence skills.

647
00:32:20.840 --> 00:32:22.640
So these are skills that do not have

648
00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:26.400
some basic safety instructions inside of them.

649
00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:29.720
For example: check this into a repository.

650
00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:32.280
Do not make it a public repository.

651
00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:35.200
If you cannot commit to the repository, fail.

652
00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:37.800
Don't like go off and sort of send it in another way.

653
00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:40.680
So a bunch of these types of examples are very real examples.

654
00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:43.680
And we have cases where we've had agents.

655
00:32:43.880 --> 00:32:46.720
There's a behavior called reward seeking which is you ask them to do something

656
00:32:46.720 --> 00:32:48.680
and they're really, really, really keen to do it.

657
00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:51.000
And so they go up and they try to do everything they can,

658
00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:52.280
and they escape their sandbox

659
00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:55.360
and they delete files and they do whatever it is to please you.

660
00:32:55.560 --> 00:32:58.280
And so you have to define a little bit of these safety instructions.

661
00:32:58.280 --> 00:33:01.080
It's kind of similar to what Brian's example was on.

662
00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:05.080
Right to say do not disclose information that makes it at least less negligent.

663
00:33:06.040 --> 00:33:09.720
And then again, similar to software, there's a question about supply chain.

664
00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:11.840
How do you consume these skills today?

665
00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:15.320
The reality is that people just consume them out of GitHub repo.

666
00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:17.880
They download them from wherever. You have no idea that it happened.

667
00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:18.920
You have no idea where it's there.

668
00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:21.200
Then they check them in to their repositories.

669
00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:22.800
Different agents read them from different places,

670
00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:26.400
so you might check them in seven times the different folders within your repo.

671
00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:27.280
It's not.

672
00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:28.960
It's early, it's fine, will improve.

673
00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:30.160
But for now it's not awesome.

674
00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:31.000
So you have to think a little bit

675
00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:32.680
about supply chain. So all of those become obvious

676
00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:35.720
once you think about skills as units of software.

677
00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:38.040
So what do we want to do here.

678
00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:41.320
Like what is what is enterprise grade

679
00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:44.000
kind of skill governance skill usage on it.

680
00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:45.120
This is a nascent space.

681
00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:47.760
I'll give you the lens on it because this is our world.

682
00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:48.960
We think you need to think about

683
00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:52.520
three different elements of of evolving this piece of software.

684
00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:54.160
First is governance and security.

685
00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:55.840
Know what the hell is going on?

686
00:33:55.840 --> 00:33:59.560
Try to sort of audit the use of it — like, you've posted skills.

687
00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:01.160
Did anybody install them?

688
00:34:01.160 --> 00:34:03.080
Constrain the use of skills.

689
00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:06.880
So people download skills always through this sort of centralized path.

690
00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:08.000
Again, not that dissimilar

691
00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:11.080
to what you should be doing with NPM libraries or whatever.

692
00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:12.600
And so you have to know about the governance.

693
00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:15.400
If you can't do that, you really oftentimes cannot roll out.

694
00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:19.480
Once you rolled out, you have a need to standardize and allow reuse.

695
00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:22.160
If three different people created a skill

696
00:34:22.160 --> 00:34:24.800
to review code, and a fourth person comes along and says,

697
00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:27.800
I want to use a skill like, first of all, where do they find it?

698
00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:30.360
Second is, how do they know which of the three to change?

699
00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:32.360
If I created a skill and then one of you came in

700
00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:35.360
and proposed the modification, how do you know if it's good or not good.

701
00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:38.760
If I now 100 people are using this skill and I'm

702
00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:42.520
going to make a change to the skill, how do I know that I'm not breaking it?

703
00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:44.200
And so there's a bunch of this notion

704
00:34:44.200 --> 00:34:48.360
of standardization, of reusability that you have to create a measure.

705
00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:51.840
And then lastly, and this is the holy grail is continuous optimization.

706
00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:57.800
You want to know that this is that CDLC — that we want the continuous optimisation.

707
00:34:57.800 --> 00:34:59.200
You want to observe what has happened.

708
00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:00.120
Did the agent fail?

709
00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:04.480
Did the user need to correct the agent and take that information

710
00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:08.360
and route that back to be able to evolve the skill, create new eval scenarios?

711
00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:09.440
And that is the holy grail.

712
00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:11.880
And the companies that are at the cutting edge are doing this right.

713
00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.520
They are creating that optimization.

714
00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:15.760
Most organizations are quite far from it.

715
00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:18.640
And so that's why this is oftentimes the sequence.

716
00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:20.840
I'd be

717
00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:24.120
remiss if I didn't do a little bit of a Tessl plug over here.

718
00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:25.760
So that's oftentimes what we help you do.

719
00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:28.760
We have a platform in which, on one hand from a

720
00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:32.160
from a development perspective, we help you collaboratively develop skills

721
00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:36.160
that allow developers to discover and install those quality skills

722
00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:40.960
and then observe what has happened, learn from that and create new paths.

723
00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:45.080
And then within that we have these these controls.

724
00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:49.600
So we have the ability to now make sure with Snyk we scan every skill

725
00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:52.640
that gets published into the into the registry.

726
00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:54.680
So you know that it's not malicious.

727
00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:57.560
Similarly, when you install we have controls about scan from us.

728
00:35:57.560 --> 00:35:59.520
We have analytics about who's using what.

729
00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:03.240
And then lastly for the sort of these nascent agent enablement

730
00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:06.560
teams, these platform teams, developer experience team AI enablement teams

731
00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:10.120
that are that own successful rollout of agents in the organization.

732
00:36:10.120 --> 00:36:11.480
We give them a bunch of these abilities

733
00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:15.200
to eliminate duplicates, drive skill usage, optimize costs.

734
00:36:15.520 --> 00:36:18.320
I like to say that agentic development is cheaper than expensive.

735
00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:19.440
It's like very cheap at the beginning

